---
// CONTENT.md section 8. Six stories and one table. Each story leads with a
// plain-English hook and puts the technical detail underneath.
// --color-withdrawn is only ever used here and on withdrawn measurement values.
import DataTable from '../components/DataTable.astro';
import Measurement from '../components/Measurement.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';

const perf = [
  ['Missing foreign key index', '1,140 ms', '0.608 ms, 1,875x faster'],
  ['Deduplication comparing every paper against every other, with no index on the comparison', '11 minutes without finishing one strategy', 'all four strategies in seconds'],
  ['Fuzzy text matching reading its cutoff from a global setting and ignoring the one in the query', 'timeout at 25 minutes', 'seconds'],
  ['Undo-snapshot query scanning 191K rows per merge group instead of looking up one key', '3.3 hours', 'minutes'],
  ['Shared memory left at a 64 MB default against a roughly 500 MB index build', 'failed instantly at minute zero', '36 seconds once raised to 2 GB'],
];
---
<Shell
  title="Bugs that passed their own tests"
  description="Bugs that did not crash. A test that passed because it asked the wrong question, a benchmark reporting a number it could not have measured, and what changed afterwards."
>
  <SectionHeading as="h1" solid="Bugs That Passed" ghost="Their Own Tests" />
  <p class="display-lede">
    Most of my worst bugs were not crashes. They were things that looked like
    they worked: a test that passed because it was asking the wrong question, a
    benchmark reporting a number it could not possibly have measured. Here are
    the ones worth writing down. The habit of asking whether a result could have
    failed is the most useful thing I have picked up so far.
  </p>

  <section class="story" aria-labelledby="s1">
    <h2 class="sub-h" id="s1">The test that could not fail</h2>
    <p class="hook">
      The program reported it had written 2,304 rows. The database had none. The
      test written to catch exactly this was green.
    </p>
    <p>
      psycopg connections default to implicit-transaction mode, where an open
      transaction turns every <code>with conn.transaction():</code> into a
      savepoint. So every per-batch commit rode inside one giant transaction
      that died with the process.
    </p>
    <p>
      The test passed because it checked the data through the writer's own
      connection, and a connection always sees its own uncommitted work. It was
      asking the wrong question.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fix: <code>autocommit=True</code> at job entrypoints, and both
      <code>backfill()</code> and <code>ingest()</code> now raise on a
      default-mode connection. The new tests verify durability from a second
      connection with the dying writer closed uncommitted. I stash-swapped the
      fix back out to confirm the new tests actually fail against the broken
      code. The ingest loop had the identical latent defect, so the guarantee
      that a crash loses at most one item had been false the entire time. Had I
      not caught it, the 10-hour encode would have committed nothing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="story" aria-labelledby="s2">
    <h2 class="sub-h" id="s2">Proving it survives being killed</h2>
    <p class="hook">
      Claiming a job can resume after a crash is easy. I killed it mid-run and
      then compared every row byte for byte.
    </p>
    <p>
      Killed the encoder mid-batch at "embedded 2304", exit 137. A fresh
      connection saw exactly 2,304 durable rows and the uncommitted batch was
      correctly lost. Resume wrote exactly 2,696 more with no double work. All
      2,304 pre-kill rows were byte-identical after resume, verified by md5 diff
      of every row, output empty. The design reason it works: the work queue is
      simply "rows that have no vector yet", so there is no checkpoint file that
      can drift out of step with the database.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="story" aria-labelledby="s3">
    <h2 class="sub-h" id="s3">A maximum wearing a percentile's name</h2>
    <p class="hook">
      I published a worst-case speed figure that was arithmetically incapable of
      being one. It hid 38 percent of the real tail.
    </p>
    <p>
      The figure was computed from 20 samples. With that few, the worst-case
      measure just returns the largest value you happened to record, so it was a
      maximum wearing a percentile's name. The real figure was 136.7 ms against
      the 98.7 ms I had published.
    </p>
    <div class="withdrawn-pair">
      <Measurement
        label="worst-case query latency"
        value="98.7"
        unit="ms"
        state="withdrawn"
        reason="Computed from 20 samples, so the measure returned the largest observed value rather than a percentile. The real figure was 136.7 ms, so 38 percent of the tail was hidden."
      />
      <Measurement
        label="end-to-end retrieval speedup"
        value="5.5"
        unit="x"
        state="withdrawn"
        reason="A speedup ratio that timed only part of the work on top and all of it underneath. It understated the real result: 5.5x reported against 6.3x actual."
      />
    </div>
    <p>
      The benchmark harness now refuses rather than guesses. Where a figure is
      not point-estimable it returns nothing and states the observed range
      instead, which is what the worst-case block on the Sieve page does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="story" aria-labelledby="s4">
    <h2 class="sub-h" id="s4">The bill that quietly doubled</h2>
    <p class="hook">
      A paid API charged me twice for the same work. No test caught it, because
      no test was watching the money.
    </p>
    <p>
      The budget check ran after the page generator had already fetched, and
      been billed for, the next page. Five works cost 64 credits. After the fix
      an identical rerun cost 32, and both figures reconciled exactly with the
      server's own accounting. It was found by cost instrumentation I added
      deliberately, not by any test.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="story" aria-labelledby="s5">
    <h2 class="sub-h" id="s5">Reading the data by hand</h2>
    <p class="hook">
      Thousands of papers had no real abstract, just repeated boilerplate. Five
      separate times, reading the actual rows overturned a rule that looked
      correct.
    </p>
    <p>
      801 papers whose entire abstract is the string "International audience", a
      repository deposit artifact. 442 reading "Monthly data release from
      WikiPathways.org". 129 sharing an ethics disclaimer. For those papers the
      system had stored the meaning of the boilerplate rather than the meaning
      of the paper. Fix was a blocklist table, 932 hashes covering 3,847 papers,
      which are indexed on their title alone. I chose a table rather than a
      computed rule because "shared across N papers" is derived from the current
      collection, and a rule would silently change a paper's treatment as new
      sources landed.
    </p>
    <p>
      23 boilerplate abstracts turned out to be 59, and roughly two thirds of
      those were legitimate. Titles differing by a single digit, "Additional
      file 5" against "Additional file 7", scored 0.97 similarity, and they were
      79 percent of the population in exactly the band a strict cutoff trusts
      most. A parent paper and its own supplementary file matched at 0.921, in a
      form the rule was structurally blind to, and the regression test asserting
      this could never happen had been passing for the wrong reason. "Preprint
      (Japanese) (AI-Ready)" and a Zenodo error message both sailed past a
      minimum-length guard, because generic strings can be long. And a
      legitimate textbook description, correctly kept off the blocklist, turned
      out to merge 23 distinct chapters into one paper.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="conclusion">
      <p>
        Records that are parts of, or versions of, a common parent inherit the
        parent's abstract. That is a permanent property of scholarly metadata,
        not an enumerable list of bad strings, so blocklisting treats symptoms.
        The structural fix was one condition: same abstract plus same title is a
        duplicate, same abstract plus different titles is siblings under a
        shared parent.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="story" aria-labelledby="s6">
    <h2 class="sub-h" id="s6">The optimisation I measured and declined</h2>
    <p class="hook">
      A change made the slowest step 2.2 times faster. I did not ship it,
      because the evidence did not show what it appeared to show.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storing each paper's numeric representation at lower precision measured 29
      documents per second against 13, with similarity to the full-precision
      versions at a mean of 0.9977. But similarity between two versions of the
      same document does not establish that the ordering of search results is
      preserved, because ordering depends on relative distances across the whole
      collection and small changes reorder near-ties. The correct test is
      whether the top 10 results still match the full-precision index, and that
      needs the full-precision index to exist first. So it is deferred as a
      measured optimisation, not a rejected one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="story" aria-labelledby="s7">
    <h2 class="sub-h" id="s7">Five that were only slow</h2>
    <p class="hook">
      These ones did hang, or time out, or fail outright. They were the easy
      kind.
    </p>
    <DataTable
      caption="Performance bugs, before and after"
      columns={['bug', 'before', 'after']}
      rows={perf}
    />
  </section>
</Shell>

<style>
  .story {
    margin-top: 4.5rem;
  }

  .story p {
    max-width: 68ch;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
  }

  .hook {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .withdrawn-pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .withdrawn-pair {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    background-color: var(--color-paper-2);
    /* Full-strength ink, never inherited. Inline code sits on the recessed
       surface, and inheriting a provenance caption's ink-3 there is the one
       place ink-3 could land on the darkest surface and fail AA. */
    color: var(--color-ink);
    padding: 0.1em 0.3em;
    border-radius: var(--radius-control);
  }

  .conclusion {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    background-color: var(--color-plot-soft);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    max-width: 68ch;
  }

  .conclusion p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
</style>
